==Finnish==
[[File:Wolverine in Sweden.jpg|thumb]]

===Etymology===
From {{inh|fi|urj-fin-pro|*ahma||glutton (one who eats a lot)}}. Related to dialectal {{cog|et|ahm}}, {{cog|izh|ahmo}}, {{cog|krl|ahmo}}, {{m|krl|ahmoi}}, {{cog|lud|ahmo}}, {{cog|vot|ahmia}} (~ {{m|fi|ahmia}}) and more distantly to {{cog|se|vuosmmis}} (from {{cog|smi-pro|*vuosvēs}}). Probably ultimately borrowed from {{der|fi|iir-pro|*Haćman-}} (compare {{cog|sa|अश्मन्||eater}}).<ref>{{R:fi:EVE|ahma|accessdate=2024-01-01}}</ref>

The meaning "wolverine" is possibly a calque from other European languages such as {{ncog|de|Vielfraß}} and {{ncog|la|gulo}}. Alternatively, according to SSA, the shift {{m-g|glutton}} &rarr; {{m-g|wolverine}} may have first occurred in Finnish and been calqued into Low German and thence into other languages.<ref>{{R:fi:SSA|ahma}}</ref>

